1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,499
Ravi did exactly what he says.
He pulled. He edited one file.

2
00:00:08,539 --> 00:00:12,074
He ran git commit -a with a
message about chunk sizes.

3
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:17,570
And his commit contains two changes: his
one line, and a second line that moves the

4
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:20,483
shared library back to
last month's version.

5
00:00:20,823 --> 00:00:24,461
He never touched the library. He
never ran a submodule command.

6
00:00:24,501 --> 00:00:29,131
The downgrade was sitting in his working
tree from the moment the pull finished,

7
00:00:29,171 --> 00:00:33,626
shown by git status as "modified", and
git commit -a stages everything that's

8
00:00:33,666 --> 00:00:34,151
modified.

9
00:00:34,491 --> 00:00:39,541
After a push, that becomes the fourth report
on the list: the service silently runs

10
00:00:39,581 --> 00:00:44,386
the old library again, and CI on main
goes red over a directory nobody touched.

11
00:00:44,426 --> 00:00:48,924
So how did it get there? Today you
watch it happen, one command at a time.

12
00:00:50,764 --> 00:00:54,305
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:54,345 --> 00:00:59,236
In the last video you saw a submodule at
rest: another repository checked out inside

14
00:00:59,276 --> 00:01:03,873
yours, with a gitlink that records one
commit ID, a URL, and a repository under

15
00:01:03,913 --> 00:01:08,452
.git/modules. You learned to compare
three values: the commit the superproject

16
00:01:08,492 --> 00:01:13,324
records, the commit checked out in the
submodule, and the commits that exist in the

17
00:01:13,364 --> 00:01:14,381
shared repository.

18
00:01:14,721 --> 00:01:19,629
Today those values move, and they move
separately. First inside the submodule, where

19
00:01:19,669 --> 00:01:24,165
HEAD is detached. Then in the superproject,
when someone moves the pointer on

20
00:01:24,205 --> 00:01:28,818
purpose. Then on a teammate's machine,
where a pull moves one value and not the

21
00:01:28,858 --> 00:01:29,171
other.

22
00:01:31,011 --> 00:01:35,664
explain why HEAD is detached in a submodule
after update, and why that is correct;

23
00:01:36,004 --> 00:01:38,750
commit inside a submodule
without losing the commit;

24
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:43,592
move the recorded pointer and read
the change in git status and git diff

25
00:01:43,632 --> 00:01:44,349
--submodule;

26
00:01:44,689 --> 00:01:49,567
explain how a teammate's stale submodule
leads to a commit that rolls the pointer

27
00:01:49,607 --> 00:01:49,870
back;

28
00:01:50,210 --> 00:01:53,537
configure a clone so that
pulls keep submodules in step.

29
00:01:55,377 --> 00:01:58,783
The detached HEAD. The
superproject asked for a commit.

30
00:01:58,823 --> 00:02:03,420
It didn't ask for a branch, and it
can't: the gitlink has no room for one.

31
00:02:03,460 --> 00:02:08,432
So git submodule update does what git
switch --detach <commit> does: HEAD points

32
00:02:08,472 --> 00:02:13,633
straight at a commit, not at a branch. A
local main exists in the submodule because

33
00:02:13,673 --> 00:02:18,771
the clone created it. HEAD isn't on it.
This is the correct state for a dependency

34
00:02:18,811 --> 00:02:19,836
you only consume.

35
00:02:20,176 --> 00:02:23,515
It becomes a trap the moment
you edit the library in place.

36
00:02:23,555 --> 00:02:25,520
A commit made there's on no branch.

37
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:30,470
The next git submodule update checks out
the commit the superproject records, and

38
00:02:30,510 --> 00:02:33,053
says nothing about the
commit it left behind.

39
00:02:33,393 --> 00:02:38,280
The commit isn't lost: the submodule is a
repository with its own reflog, its local

40
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:42,375
journal of where HEAD has been. A
branch can anchor the commit again.

41
00:02:42,415 --> 00:02:47,242
The prevention is a habit: before you change
anything inside a submodule, get on a

42
00:02:47,282 --> 00:02:48,014
branch there.

43
00:02:48,354 --> 00:02:53,716
For people who develop the library inside
the superproject, git submodule update

44
00:02:53,756 --> 00:02:59,253
--merge or --rebase integrates the recorded
commit into the submodule's branch and

45
00:02:59,293 --> 00:03:03,778
leaves HEAD attached. For consumers,
the default checkout is right.

46
00:03:04,118 --> 00:03:08,872
Moving the pointer. Nothing changes in
the superproject until someone moves the

47
00:03:08,912 --> 00:03:13,605
gitlink. 🟡 CAUTION: git submodule update
--remote fetches in the submodule and

48
00:03:13,645 --> 00:03:18,460
checks out the tip of its remote-tracking
branch instead of the recorded commit.

49
00:03:18,500 --> 00:03:22,161
Which branch? By default the one
the remote's HEAD points at.

50
00:03:22,201 --> 00:03:25,560
git submodule set-branch
records another in .gitmodules.

51
00:03:25,600 --> 00:03:29,201
That setting is read by update
--remote and by nothing else.

52
00:03:29,241 --> 00:03:34,177
It doesn't make the submodule "follow" a
branch, and the gitlink is still a commit

53
00:03:34,217 --> 00:03:34,359
ID.

54
00:03:34,699 --> 00:03:39,247
Recording the new pointer is an ordinary
add and commit in the superproject.

55
00:03:39,287 --> 00:03:44,017
Treat it like any dependency bump: one
purpose, its own pull request, CI on the

56
00:03:44,057 --> 00:03:44,439
result.

57
00:03:44,779 --> 00:03:49,610
The stale submodule. git pull is fetch
plus merge. The fetch notices that the new

58
00:03:49,650 --> 00:03:53,579
commits change a gitlink and fetches
inside the submodule as well.

59
00:03:53,619 --> 00:03:57,368
That on-demand fetch is the default
of fetch.recurseSubmodules.

60
00:03:57,408 --> 00:04:00,856
The merge updates the gitlink
in the superproject's index.

61
00:04:00,896 --> 00:04:04,043
It doesn't check out the new
commit in the submodule.

62
00:04:04,083 --> 00:04:08,734
Checking out a superproject commit doesn't
move the HEAD of a submodule unless

63
00:04:08,774 --> 00:04:09,696
recursion is on.

64
00:04:10,036 --> 00:04:14,843
Why does Git behave this way? The submodule
may hold work in progress, and Git won't

65
00:04:14,883 --> 00:04:16,458
move it without being asked.

66
00:04:16,798 --> 00:04:22,061
The cure. git config set submodule.recurse
true. With it, checkout, fetch, grep,

67
00:04:22,101 --> 00:04:27,496
pull, push, read-tree, reset, restore and
switch behave as if --recurse-submodules

68
00:04:27,536 --> 00:04:32,931
were given. git clone is the exception: it
needs its own --recurse-submodules, and

69
00:04:32,971 --> 00:04:38,101
so does git ls-files. The manual's workflow
section recommends the setting for

70
00:04:38,141 --> 00:04:43,602
repositories that are split into submodules.
It's local or global configuration, so

71
00:04:43,642 --> 00:04:49,037
it can't be shipped with the repository:
every clone, and every CI job, has to set

72
00:04:49,077 --> 00:04:50,496
it or pass the option.

73
00:04:52,336 --> 00:04:56,251
Return to the recipe that cites
page 212 of the other book.

74
00:04:56,291 --> 00:04:58,597
Three values, written side by side:

75
00:04:58,937 --> 00:05:03,414
What your book cites. What page the
reader's copy of the other book is open at.

76
00:05:03,454 --> 00:05:05,529
What the bookshop's edition contains.

77
00:05:05,869 --> 00:05:10,509
Every command today changes one of the
three. git submodule update opens the

78
00:05:10,549 --> 00:05:15,496
reader's copy at the page your book cites.
update --remote opens it at the newest

79
00:05:15,536 --> 00:05:20,544
page in the shop. git add on the submodule
path rewrites the citation in your book

80
00:05:20,584 --> 00:05:25,655
to wherever the copy is open. And git pull
brings a new edition of your book with a

81
00:05:25,695 --> 00:05:29,411
new citation, and leaves the
reader's copy open where it was.

82
00:05:29,751 --> 00:05:34,503
The dangerous step follows from that. A
reader who then runs "write down where my

83
00:05:34,543 --> 00:05:39,237
copy is open", which is what git commit
-a does, puts the old page back into the

84
00:05:39,277 --> 00:05:42,668
book. Where the analogy breaks:
nobody decided to do that.

85
00:05:42,708 --> 00:05:45,390
It was the default staging
of a modified path.

86
00:05:45,730 --> 00:05:50,929
Try it now, on paper, thirty seconds. Two
columns: recorded in the superproject, and

87
00:05:50,969 --> 00:05:56,044
checked out in the submodule. For Ravi's
clone right after his git pull, write new

88
00:05:56,084 --> 00:05:58,476
or old in each. That's your prediction.

89
00:06:04,816 --> 00:06:08,848
Check your paper against row two:
recorded is new, checked out is old.

90
00:06:09,188 --> 00:06:14,322
Now read row three slowly. After the bad
commit the two values agree again, and the

91
00:06:14,362 --> 00:06:17,752
status prefix is a space. The
repository looks healthy.

92
00:06:17,792 --> 00:06:21,742
The damage is in the history: the
recorded value went backwards.

93
00:06:22,082 --> 00:06:27,142
Read the root-cause line of the box: two
repositories, two HEADs, and no default

94
00:06:27,182 --> 00:06:29,309
that ties the second to the first.

95
00:06:31,149 --> 00:06:36,154
"HEAD detached at e216665". A local
main exists, and HEAD isn't on it.

96
00:06:36,494 --> 00:06:40,910
The commit 679aa20 is decorated
with HEAD only. No branch.

97
00:06:40,950 --> 00:06:46,979
Meanwhile you have moved the pointer to
the library's 0.2.0 release and pushed.

98
00:06:47,019 --> 00:06:51,588
Ravi pulls and updates. Predict
what Git says about 679aa20.

99
00:06:51,628 --> 00:06:53,662
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

100
00:06:58,502 --> 00:07:02,915
Nothing. git submodule update checked
out the commit that the superproject now

101
00:07:02,955 --> 00:07:07,596
records. The rescue is the one from the
recovery module, run inside the submodule.

102
00:07:07,936 --> 00:07:11,779
The submodule's own HEAD reflog
has the commit at HEAD@{1}.

103
00:07:11,819 --> 00:07:15,201
A branch anchors it. And
the habit that prevents it:

104
00:07:15,541 --> 00:07:20,529
On a branch inside the submodule. The
superproject shows the path as modified, and

105
00:07:20,569 --> 00:07:25,557
the status prefix is a plus sign: the
checked-out commit differs from the recorded

106
00:07:25,597 --> 00:07:25,803
one.

107
00:07:28,643 --> 00:07:31,001
The prefix went from a space to a plus.

108
00:07:31,341 --> 00:07:39,122
The submodule is at b3c86ce, tagged v0.2.0.
The superproject still records e216665.

109
00:07:39,462 --> 00:07:44,686
The words "new commits" in git status mean:
the submodule's HEAD differs from the

110
00:07:44,726 --> 00:07:50,014
gitlink in the index. git diff --submodule=log
replaces the two IDs by the list of

111
00:07:50,054 --> 00:07:53,978
commits between them, which is
what a reviewer wants to read.

112
00:07:54,018 --> 00:07:57,553
git config set diff.submodule
log makes it the default.

113
00:07:57,893 --> 00:08:03,097
One file changed, one insertion, one
deletion: the one-line "Subproject commit"

114
00:08:03,137 --> 00:08:04,623
file. Now Ravi's clone.

115
00:08:04,963 --> 00:08:08,298
Ravi runs git pull. He has
made no change of his own.

116
00:08:08,338 --> 00:08:12,309
Predict the first character of
git submodule status afterwards.

117
00:08:12,349 --> 00:08:12,946
I'll wait.

118
00:08:20,786 --> 00:08:24,187
Read the output in order. The
superproject was fetched.

119
00:08:24,227 --> 00:08:29,004
"Fetching submodule vendor/textsplit":
the new library commit was downloaded.

120
00:08:29,044 --> 00:08:34,009
Then the superproject was fast-forwarded.
No line says the submodule was checked

121
00:08:34,049 --> 00:08:35,447
out, because it wasn't.

122
00:08:35,787 --> 00:08:37,249
A plus, and the word "rewind".

123
00:08:37,589 --> 00:08:42,856
The superproject now records b3c86ce.
The submodule's working tree is still at

124
00:08:42,896 --> 00:08:48,232
e216665. Seen from the superproject, the
working tree differs from the index by

125
00:08:48,272 --> 00:08:53,336
going one library commit backwards.
This is row two of the diagram, and the

126
00:08:53,376 --> 00:08:54,356
root-cause box.

127
00:08:54,696 --> 00:08:59,938
The correct next command is git submodule
update. Ravi doesn't look closely.

128
00:08:59,978 --> 00:09:03,691
Quick quiz: he edits ingest.py
and runs git commit -a.

129
00:09:03,731 --> 00:09:07,652
Will his commit change one
file, or two? Say it out loud.

130
00:09:12,492 --> 00:09:16,471
Two files changed: ingest.py,
and vendor/textsplit.

131
00:09:16,811 --> 00:09:21,831
His commit b98f829 moved the gitlink back.
That's the downgrade from the opening.

132
00:09:21,871 --> 00:09:26,766
A reviewer sees it only as one changed
line in a commit that claims to be about

133
00:09:26,806 --> 00:09:27,516
chunk sizes.

134
00:09:27,856 --> 00:09:31,494
The commit hasn't been pushed,
so it can be repaired in place.

135
00:09:31,534 --> 00:09:34,698
🟡 CAUTION: git commit --amend
replaces the tip commit.

136
00:09:34,738 --> 00:09:36,596
The old one stays in the reflog.

137
00:09:36,936 --> 00:09:42,027
origin/main:vendor/textsplit is the gitlink
as the remote-tracking branch records

138
00:09:42,067 --> 00:09:47,222
it. git rev-parse returns the ID even though
the object isn't in the superproject.

139
00:09:47,562 --> 00:09:52,742
After the amend, the commit changes one
file, and 2b86667 has taken the place of

140
00:09:52,782 --> 00:09:56,591
b98f829. The script uses git
checkout inside the submodule.

141
00:09:56,631 --> 00:10:00,441
git switch --detach with the
same ID is the newer spelling.

142
00:10:00,481 --> 00:10:05,727
If the bad commit had already been pushed,
the fix is a new commit that moves the

143
00:10:05,767 --> 00:10:07,162
pointer forward again.

144
00:10:07,502 --> 00:10:11,899
And the same staleness appears with every
command that moves the superproject's

145
00:10:11,939 --> 00:10:12,180
HEAD.

146
00:10:12,520 --> 00:10:16,024
An older commit of doc-qa records
the older library commit.

147
00:10:16,064 --> 00:10:20,648
Without recursion the submodule stays
where it was, and the prefix is a plus.

148
00:10:20,988 --> 00:10:25,887
With submodule.recurse set, the submodule
follows the superproject in both

149
00:10:25,927 --> 00:10:31,294
directions, and the prefix stays a space.
🟡 CAUTION: this is a configuration key,

150
00:10:31,334 --> 00:10:35,231
written with git config set,
which needs Git 2.46 or later.

151
00:10:35,271 --> 00:10:37,100
It lives in this clone only.

152
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:40,588
Five mistakes to watch for.

153
00:10:40,928 --> 00:10:45,737
Committing inside a submodule without a
branch. Root cause: update leaves HEAD

154
00:10:45,777 --> 00:10:50,648
detached at the recorded commit, so the
new commit is on no branch and the next

155
00:10:50,688 --> 00:10:52,824
update moves away from it silently.

156
00:10:53,164 --> 00:10:56,271
Expecting git pull to update
the submodule's files.

157
00:10:56,311 --> 00:11:01,392
Root cause: pull fetches in the submodule
and updates the gitlink in the index, and

158
00:11:01,432 --> 00:11:04,971
does not move the submodule's
HEAD unless recursion is on.

159
00:11:05,311 --> 00:11:10,421
git commit -a with a stale submodule.
Root cause: the stale checkout shows as a

160
00:11:10,461 --> 00:11:15,702
modified path, and -a stages every modified
path, which records the old commit ID

161
00:11:15,742 --> 00:11:16,093
again.

162
00:11:16,433 --> 00:11:20,841
Thinking submodule.<name>.branch
makes the submodule follow a branch.

163
00:11:20,881 --> 00:11:26,192
Root cause: the setting is read by update
--remote and by nothing else; the gitlink

164
00:11:26,232 --> 00:11:27,546
is still a commit ID.

165
00:11:27,886 --> 00:11:31,664
Setting submodule.recurse once
and assuming the team has it.

166
00:11:31,704 --> 00:11:36,627
Root cause: it is local or global
configuration and cannot be shipped with the

167
00:11:36,667 --> 00:11:40,127
repository; every clone and
every CI job has to set it.

168
00:11:41,967 --> 00:11:47,135
Now, out of the lab. A retrieval team
vendors a chunking library as a submodule in

169
00:11:47,175 --> 00:11:51,835
three services. Twice in one month a
feature pull request has contained an

170
00:11:51,875 --> 00:11:56,789
unexplained second changed line,
vendor/textsplit, and once it was merged: the

171
00:11:56,829 --> 00:12:00,218
service ran the previous
library version for two days.

172
00:12:00,558 --> 00:12:05,698
The team changes three things. The setup
script that every engineer and every CI job

173
00:12:05,738 --> 00:12:10,755
runs sets submodule.recurse to true and
diff.submodule to log, so a pull keeps the

174
00:12:10,795 --> 00:12:14,516
submodule in step and a pointer
change is readable in review.

175
00:12:14,556 --> 00:12:18,956
Pointer changes get their own pull
request, titled as a dependency bump.

176
00:12:18,996 --> 00:12:23,704
And reviewers are told what to look for:
a "Subproject commit" line in a pull

177
00:12:23,744 --> 00:12:28,576
request that isn't about the dependency,
and the word "rewind" in the submodule

178
00:12:28,616 --> 00:12:28,884
diff.

179
00:12:30,724 --> 00:12:36,771
Your turn. Do Lab 15.1, "A submodule
that breaks for a teammate", in

180
00:12:36,811 --> 00:12:40,531
lab-manual/m15-submodules-subtrees-lfs.md.

181
00:12:40,871 --> 00:12:45,377
Keep a three-column table as you go:
recorded, checked out, status prefix.

182
00:12:45,417 --> 00:12:49,492
Before every command, predict which
of the three values it changes.

183
00:12:49,532 --> 00:12:53,730
Before the teammate's commit, predict
how many files it will contain.

184
00:12:54,070 --> 00:13:00,734
The challenge is Exercise 15.4, Level 2,
command prediction, "The first character of

185
00:13:00,774 --> 00:13:06,321
git submodule status", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

186
00:13:10,161 --> 00:13:15,120
Answer out loud. A strong answer derives
the detached HEAD from what the gitlink can

187
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:19,405
and can't hold, and says why that
state is the right one for a consumer.

188
00:13:19,445 --> 00:13:24,405
It then describes the exact sequence in
which work appears to vanish, and is precise

189
00:13:24,445 --> 00:13:27,857
that the commit is unreachable
from branches, not deleted.

190
00:13:28,197 --> 00:13:32,421
It names where the evidence is, which
belongs to the submodule and not to the

191
00:13:32,461 --> 00:13:35,909
superproject, and gives the
two-step rescue: find, then anchor.

192
00:13:35,949 --> 00:13:40,395
It ends with the habit that prevents it,
and with the update modes for people who

193
00:13:40,435 --> 00:13:42,389
really develop the library in place.

194
00:13:44,229 --> 00:13:49,042
Let's land this. One last look inside the
submodule: a commit left behind, and the

195
00:13:49,082 --> 00:13:50,404
branch that anchors it.

196
00:13:50,744 --> 00:13:52,404
You should now be able to say:

197
00:13:52,744 --> 00:13:57,424
After update, a submodule's HEAD is
detached because the superproject records a

198
00:13:57,464 --> 00:13:58,679
commit, not a branch.

199
00:13:59,019 --> 00:14:03,888
Before editing inside a submodule I switch
to a branch there; a commit left behind

200
00:14:03,928 --> 00:14:05,864
is in the submodule's own reflog.

201
00:14:06,204 --> 00:14:10,450
Moving the pointer is update --remote,
then git add and a commit in the

202
00:14:10,490 --> 00:14:13,105
superproject, reviewed
as a dependency bump.

203
00:14:13,445 --> 00:14:18,563
A pull updates the recorded ID and leaves
the submodule's checkout stale; git commit

204
00:14:18,603 --> 00:14:21,019
-a in that state rolls the pointer back.

205
00:14:21,359 --> 00:14:27,878
submodule.recurse=true keeps the checkout
in step, and every clone and CI job has to

206
00:14:27,918 --> 00:14:28,424
set it.

207
00:14:30,264 --> 00:14:35,120
Read sections 23.5 to 23.7 of Chapter 23.

208
00:14:35,460 --> 00:14:40,489
Today you watched a silent rollback form,
one value at a time, and you repaired it.

209
00:14:40,529 --> 00:14:44,703
Practise with the lab and your
three-column table before you move on.

210
00:14:44,743 --> 00:14:47,939
Next time: submodule failures,
from push order to CI.

211
00:14:47,979 --> 00:14:51,115
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

212
00:14:51,155 --> 00:14:52,581
See you in the next one.
